{"id":"def:minimax-risk","name":"public-experiment-indexed exact-shell minimax risk","construction":"For a fixed public experiment \(\mathfrak E\) and every nonempty class \(\mathcal M\) of laws compatible with \(\mathfrak E\), use the standard extended reverse-KL convention in \(J_{\eta,P}\). Thus a policy that assigns positive mass outside \(\operatorname{supp}\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot\mid x)\) at any context with positive \(\rho\)-mass has objective \(-\infty\) and regret \(+\infty\). For an arbitrary policy \(\pi\), define the public contextwise support repair
\[
(\mathscr S_{\mathrm{ref}}\pi)(a\mid x)=
\begin{cases}
\pi(a\mid x),&\pi(b\mid x)=0\text{ for every }b\text{ such that }\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(b\mid x)=0,\\
\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(a\mid x),&\text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]
Because the supports have the standing finite discrete/Borel measurable structures, \(\mathscr S_{\mathrm{ref}}\) is Borel measurable and sends every measurable policy learner to an element of \(\mathcal L_n(\mathfrak E)\). For every law and every realized learner output, its repair leaves the objective unchanged when every offending context has zero \(\rho\)-mass, while otherwise the unrepaired regret is \(+\infty\) and the repaired regret is finite. Hence repair never increases regret. Since supported learners are themselves full-simplex learners, the two infima are equal, and their common value is
\[
\mathfrak R_{n,\mathfrak E}(\mathcal M)=\inf_{\widetilde\pi\in\mathcal L_n(\mathfrak E)}\sup_{P\in\mathcal M}\mathbb E_{P^{\otimes n}}\left[J_{\eta,P}(\pi_P^\star)-J_{\eta,P}(\widetilde\pi(\mathcal D_n;\mathfrak E))\right].
\]
Equivalently, the right-hand side equals the same infimum taken over all measurable full-simplex policy-valued learners with the displayed loss interpreted in \([0,+\infty]\). The Gibbs optimum has exactly the support of \(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\), and the public reference learner has finite regret, so the common minimax value is a nonnegative real number. When \(\mathfrak E\) is fixed and no experiments are compared, write \(\mathfrak R_n=\mathfrak R_{n,\mathfrak E}\).","inputs":["\mathcal D_n","\mathfrak E","\mathcal L_n(\mathfrak E)","P^{\otimes n}","J_{\eta,P}","\pi_P^\star","\mathfrak R_{n,\mathfrak E}"]}
